  "result": "Perfect! All 107 tests pass. I've successfully implemented VAT (Value Added Tax) calculations:\n\n**Updated `src/invoices.js`:**\n- Added `lineVat()` function that calculates VAT per line:\n  - Computed on the line's amount AFTER its discount\n  - Uses `roundCents()` to round VAT per line\n  - Takes into account each line's `vatRate`\n- Updated `invoiceTotals()` to return `{ subtotal, discount, vat, total }`:\n  - `subtotal` = sum of line totals (before discount)\n  - `discount` = sum of line discounts\n  - `vat` = sum of rounded line VATs (each computed on amount after discount)\n  - `total` = subtotal - discount + vat\n\n**Updated `src/render.js`:**\n- Added a VAT line in the rendered output\n- Displayed just above the total line\n- Uses `formatEuros()` for consistent formatting\n\n**Test Coverage:**\n- Added 7 new comprehensive tests covering:\n  - `lineVat` computation with different scenarios\n  - VAT with discounts applied\n  - Different VAT rates (21%, 9%, 0%)\n  - Per-line VAT rounding (banker's rounding)\n  - VAT summing from multiple lines (sum of rounded amounts, not percentage of total)\n- Updated all existing tests to account for VAT in total calculations\n- Updated render tests to verify VAT displays correctly and line count includes VAT line\n\nAll 107 tests pass (100 previous + 7 new). The VAT calculation correctly implements the decision that VAT is computed per line on the amount after discount, rounded per line, and then summed\u2014never as a percentage of the invoice total.",
